Author: lora-dataset-studio <noreply@lora-dataset-studio.dev>
Date:   Tue Aug 25 22:16:00 2026 +0200

    fix(studio): Klein, FLUX.1 and Anima LoRAs are found where they were deployed
    
    Deploying a FLUX.2 Klein LoRA wrote it to loras/flux2klein and every later
    "is it deployed?" looked in loras/z image, so the answer was always no: the
    Canvas badge never flipped, the Generate sidebar said "not deployed yet", and
    "deploy then generate" reported success and instantly failed, on a file that
    was sitting on disk the whole time. Undeploy listed it correctly, which is
    what made the report so precise (GitHub #52, lunchingfriar).
    
    The cause was three tables naming the same set of families and drifting:
    lora_training._FAMILY_SUBDIR (six), face_dataset_service.TRAIN_TYPES (six),
    and lora_test_studio.FAMILIES (three). Klein deploy support landed a month
    ago and updated the first two. Nothing failed, because _pool_for_family's
    final `return` handed every unknown family the Z-Image pool: a wrong folder
    reads exactly like an empty one, and an empty one reads as "nothing is
    deployed". FLUX.1 and Anima were in the same hole and nobody had noticed.
    
    - FAMILIES gains flux, flux2klein and anima, which also puts them in the
      Test Studio family picker they were missing from for the same reason.
    - get_family_loras() reads a family's own folders through _lora_family_dirs,
      the same table the deploy accessors write through, so the two cannot point
      at different places again. It also picks up the extra loras roots from
      extra_model_paths.yaml, which the hand-written getters never did.
    - The silent fallback is gone: an unknown family returns [] instead of
      another family's pool. No family at all still means Z-Image.
    - test_family_pool_parity.py pins the three tables together and refuses a
      shared pool. Verified to fail against the pre-fix FAMILIES.
    
    Reported by lunchingfriar (GitHub #52), who reinstalled ComfyUI to rule out
    his own machine before filing.
